<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="ch-pre-workshop">
    <title>First steps</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Authoring in PreTeXt requires nothing more
            than a wifi connection and GitHub account.
            GitHub users also can share their
            content for free on GitHub Pages!
        </p>

        <p>
            If you prefer to use a local setup instead of the cloud-based interface, this is absolutely possible, but requires a bit more setup.  A list of the steps you will need to complete are given below, but remember, this is entirely optional.
        </p>

    </introduction>
    <section>
        <title>Setting up your GitHub account</title>
        <p>
            To create a GitHub account, 
            <url href="https://github.com/signup">
                follow the instructions on GitHub's
                signup page.
            </url>
        </p>

        <p>
            <alert>And that's it!</alert>.  Really, unless you want to get everything working locally, you can skip to <xref ref="ch-intro"/>.
        </p>
    </section>


    <section>
        <title>Apply for your GitHub Education discount</title>
        <p>
            While you can use GitHub for free, there are some features that require a <q>pro</q> account. The main advantage in this context is that the free Pro account you get access to will allow you to use GitHub Pages to publish your PreTeXt content while still keeping your repository and source files private.
        </p>
        <p>
            Educators and non-profit researchers can get a coupon for a free upgrade to the pro level.  Apply at
            <url href="https://education.github.com/discount_requests/pack_application">Education.GitHub.com</url>
            to unlock these features (in our experience, applications are usually processed quickly for <c>.edu</c>
            email addresses).
        </p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-installing-pretext-locally">
        <title>Installing PreTeXt locally</title>
        <p>
            To write in PreTeXt on your own computer, you will definitely need the following.
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A modern text editor.  We recommend <url href="https://code.visualstudio.com/">Visual Studio Code</url>.  This has the advantage of being exactly the same editor that we will use in the online Codespace workflow.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Python 3.8 or later. You can download the latest version from <url href="https://www.python.org/downloads/">python.org</url>.  <em>Make sure to check the box to add Python to your PATH during installation.</em>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Once you have Python installed, you can install PreTeXt by running the following command in your terminal or command prompt:
                        <cd>pip install pretext</cd>
                        (Depending on your system, you might need to use <c>python -m pip install pretext</c> or <c>python3 -m pip install pretext</c> instead.)
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
        <p>
            Depending on what you want to do with PreTeXt, you might need some of the following additional tools.
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A LaTeX distribution (TeX Live, MikTeX, etc.) if you want to generate PDF output or include TikZ graphics in your PreTeXt document.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        If you want to use GitHub pages or GitHub to manage your project, you will need to install Git (if you have a mac or linux system, you probably already have it installed).  You can download Git from <url href="https://git-scm.com/downloads">git-scm.com</url>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        To use any of the additional HTML themes, you will need to install Node.js and npm (Node Package Manager).  You can download Node.js from <url href="https://nodejs.org/en/download/">nodejs.org</url>. 
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        If you want to include sageplot graphics in your PreTeXt document, you will need to install SageMath.  You can download SageMath from <url href="https://www.sagemath.org/download.html">sagemath.org</url>.  
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </section>

</chapter>